    expectedAnswer: "**Button** should test its public contract: rendered state, interactions, accessibility semantics, async behavior, and important edge cases.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the topic.\n\n**Button** should test its public contract: rendered state, interactions, accessibility semantics, async behavior, and important edge cases.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nDefine the observable behavior, inputs, expected result, failure conditions, and cleanup. Use the smallest test level that provides enough confidence.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\nThe manual version exposes the core logic so it is easy to explain line by line in an interview.\n\n```ts\ninterface UiState { label: string; disabled: boolean; }\n\nfunction accessibleName(state: UiState): string {\n  if (state.label.trim().length === 0) return \"Unnamed control\";\n  return state.label.trim();\n}\n\nconst name = accessibleName({\n  label: \"Save\",\n  disabled: false,\n});\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\nUse native array/collection helpers when they make the same behavior clearer without hiding the important test idea.\n\n```ts\nconst state = { label: \"Save\", disabled: false };\n\nconst name = state.label.trim() || \"Unnamed control\";\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nTest public component behavior, rendered states, interactions, accessibility, and important edge cases.\n\nStep 6 — Test quality:\nKeep the test deterministic, isolated, diagnostic, and representative. Cover success, boundary, failure, accessibility, and cleanup where relevant.\n\nStep 7 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain why this belongs at this test level, what it deliberately does not cover, how flakiness is prevented, and how it fits the wider suite.",
